---
source: crates/leptoskit_cli/src/publish/tests.rs
expression: report.text()
---
What to do next

chrome: Commonplace 0.1.0 is built and checked; nothing was uploaded.

  1. Fill in the store listing: the description, category, and language, a 128×128 icon, and at least one 1280×800 screenshot.
     Open   https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole/commonplace-example/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/edit
     Description: Commonplace keeps the passages worth remembering from everything you read.
         
         Select a passage on any page and clip it into a research project, with a note and tags. Commonplace keeps the page's title, author, site, and date, so every clip can be cited, and highlights your clips when you come back to a page. Your library is the same on the web, on your phone, and in the extension.
     Category: Productivity
     Homepage: https://commonplace.example.com
     Support: https://commonplace.example.com/help
     Why by hand: the Chrome Web Store API uploads packages but can't edit the listing
     Done when: a submit succeeds; the store refuses one while the listing is incomplete

  2. Fill in the privacy practices: the single purpose, a justification for each permission, and the privacy policy's address.
     Open   https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole/commonplace-example/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/edit/privacy
     Single purpose: Clip quotes from anything you read into your Commonplace research projects.
     activeTab: Reads the page you're on, only when you press the toolbar button or the shortcut, so you can clip from it.
     alarms: Retries clips saved while you were offline, once a minute, until they reach your library.
     contextMenus: Adds "Clip to Commonplace" to the menu you see when you right-click selected text.
     scripting: Shows the clipper beside the text you selected, and your highlights on the sites you chose.
     sidePanel: Shows a project's clips beside the page you're reading.
     storage: Keeps your sign-in and your chosen project on this device.
     Host permission: Talks to your Commonplace server, and highlights your clips only on the sites you allow in the options.
     Privacy policy: https://commonplace.example.com/privacy
     Why by hand: the API has no privacy practices endpoint
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  3. Certify, on the same page, that the extension's data use follows the Developer Program Policies. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole/commonplace-example/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa/edit/privacy
     Why by hand: it is a legal declaration, which only the account holder can make
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  4. Decide to release it, then run `leptoskit publish chrome --submit`. (you, the account holder)
     Why by hand: submitting sends it to review and then to users, which is the account holder's decision
     Done when: the store shows the version as pending review

edge: Commonplace 0.1.0 is built and checked; nothing was uploaded.

  1. Fill in the product's Properties (category, privacy policy, website, support) and its Store listing (description and screenshots).
     Open   https://partner.microsoft.com/dashboard/microsoftedge/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/properties
     Description: Commonplace keeps the passages worth remembering from everything you read.
         
         Select a passage on any page and clip it into a research project, with a note and tags. Commonplace keeps the page's title, author, site, and date, so every clip can be cited, and highlights your clips when you come back to a page. Your library is the same on the web, on your phone, and in the extension.
     Privacy policy: https://commonplace.example.com/privacy
     Website: https://commonplace.example.com
     Support: https://commonplace.example.com/help
     Category: Productivity
     Why by hand: Edge Add-ons' Publish API uploads packages and submits them, but can't edit the listing
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  2. Decide to release it, then run `leptoskit publish edge --submit`. (you, the account holder)
     Why by hand: submitting sends it to certification and then to users
     Done when: Partner Center shows the submission in certification

firefox: Commonplace 0.1.0 is built and checked; nothing was uploaded.

  1. Fill in the listing: the description, screenshots, and privacy policy.
     Open   https://addons.mozilla.org/developers/addon/commonplace@leptoskit.dev/edit
     Description: Commonplace keeps the passages worth remembering from everything you read.
         
         Select a passage on any page and clip it into a research project, with a note and tags. Commonplace keeps the page's title, author, site, and date, so every clip can be cited, and highlights your clips when you come back to a page. Your library is the same on the web, on your phone, and in the extension.
     Privacy policy: https://commonplace.example.com/privacy
     Why by hand: leptoskit doesn't upload listing text and images yet
     Done when: the listing page shows them

  2. Decide to release it, then run `leptoskit publish firefox --submit`. (you, the account holder)
     Why by hand: a listed version goes to review and then to users
     Done when: addons.mozilla.org shows the version awaiting review

app_store: Commonplace 0.1.0 is checked; an upload archives it for team ABCDE12345, and its build number will be one more than App Store Connect's highest. Nothing was built or uploaded.

  1. Answer the export compliance question for each build in TestFlight. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/testflight/ios
     To answer it once: set ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption in ios/Info.plist, and every later build carries the answer
     Why by hand: it is a legal declaration about the app's encryption
     Done when: the build no longer shows Missing Compliance

  2. Fill in version 0.1.0's App Store page: the description, keywords, support address, and screenshots for iPhone and iPad.
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/distribution/ios/version/inflight
     Description: Commonplace keeps the passages worth remembering from everything you read.
         
         Select a passage on any page and clip it into a research project, with a note and tags. Commonplace keeps the page's title, author, site, and date, so every clip can be cited, and highlights your clips when you come back to a page. Your library is the same on the web, on your phone, and in the extension.
     Support URL: https://commonplace.example.com/help
     Marketing URL: https://commonplace.example.com
     Why by hand: leptoskit doesn't upload App Store listing text and screenshots yet
     Done when: `leptoskit publish app_store --submit` succeeds; App Review refuses an incomplete page

  3. Fill in App Information: the category, and the privacy policy's address.
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/distribution/info
     Category: Productivity
     Privacy Policy URL: https://commonplace.example.com/privacy
     Why by hand: leptoskit doesn't upload App Information yet
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  4. Answer the App Privacy questions: which data the app collects, and what for. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/distribution/privacy
     Why by hand: they are a legal declaration about the app's data, which the API can't make
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  5. Answer the age rating questions, in App Information. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/distribution/info
     Why by hand: a content rating's answers are the account holder's declaration
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  6. Give App Review a contact, and a demo account if the app needs signing in, on the version's page. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://appstoreconnect.apple.com/apps/1234567890/distribution/ios/version/inflight
     Why by hand: the contact details and the account are the account holder's own
     Done when: a submit succeeds

  7. Decide to release it, then run `leptoskit publish app_store --submit`. (you, the account holder)
     Why by hand: submitting sends it to App Review and then to users
     Done when: App Store Connect shows the version waiting for review

macos: Commonplace 0.1.0 is checked; an upload signs it with your Developer ID, notarizes it, and writes .leptoskit/publish/macos/Commonplace.dmg and .leptoskit/publish/macos/Commonplace.zip. Nothing was built or notarized.

  1. Put the disk image on the page people download the app from.
     Disk image: .leptoskit/publish/macos/Commonplace.dmg
     For updaters and scripts: .leptoskit/publish/macos/Commonplace.zip
     Why by hand: leptoskit doesn't host downloads; set [publish.macos].github_release to attach them to a GitHub release
     Done when: the page offers the new version

play: Commonplace 0.1.0 is checked; an upload builds an App Bundle signed with your upload key, numbered one more than the highest on Google Play, and puts it on the internal track as a draft. Nothing was built or uploaded.

  1. Fill in the main store listing: the app's name, short and full description, icon, feature graphic, and phone screenshots.
     Open   https://play.google.com/console
     Full description: Commonplace keeps the passages worth remembering from everything you read.
         
         Select a passage on any page and clip it into a research project, with a note and tags. Commonplace keeps the page's title, author, site, and date, so every clip can be cited, and highlights your clips when you come back to a page. Your library is the same on the web, on your phone, and in the extension.
     Website: https://commonplace.example.com
     Why by hand: leptoskit doesn't upload Google Play listings yet
     Done when: Play Console's dashboard no longer lists the store listing as a task

  2. Set the privacy policy, under App content.
     Open   https://play.google.com/console
     Privacy policy: https://commonplace.example.com/privacy
     Why by hand: the Play Developer API has no App content endpoint
     Done when: App content shows the privacy policy as done

  3. Answer App content's declarations: data safety, the content rating questionnaire, the target audience, and ads. (you, the account holder)
     Open   https://play.google.com/console
     Why by hand: they are legal declarations about the app, which only the account holder can make
     Done when: App content shows no task left

  4. Decide to release it, then run `leptoskit publish play --submit`, which releases the internal track (a staged rollout on production when `rollout` is set). (you, the account holder)
     Why by hand: releasing reaches the track's users, which is the account holder's decision
     Done when: the track's release shows as available or in progress
